  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nTuesday through Friday: Below Average Temperatures and Dry Conditions\n\nA 500mb trough will be in place over the northeast with the \ncenter of high pressure remaining over the southern High Plains \nthrough Thursday-Friday. This pattern leads to northwest flow \nover the Upper Mississippi River Valley resulting in a cooler \nair mass in place locally. This cooler air mass will initially\nbe noticeable today behind a cold frontal passage overnight. \nBelow average temperatures, in the 70s to lower 80s, are \nexpected through the work week as the cooler air mass remains \nin place. There is strong ensemble agreement in this scenario \npanning out with 25th to 75th percentile temperature differences\nonly upwards of 5 degrees through Saturday. Negative theta-e \nadvection is forecast during the rest of the week which will not\nbe favorable for the development of showers or storms.\n\nThis Weekend into Next Week: Thunderstorm Chances Return\n\nThe aforementioned upper level center of high pressure is \nexpected to move over the Intermountain West over CO/NM this \nweekend with a jet stream rounding the top of the ridge from the\nCanadian Prairies into the Midwest during this time frame. Mid \nlevel moisture should return to the region and with embedded \nH300 shortwaves progressing through the northwest flow over the \nCWA, shower and thunderstorm chances are likely to return. \nDetails are uncertain and will change as we get closer to the \nweekend, but there is the potential for severe thunderstorms on \nSunday as instability increases over the area. In addition to \nthe storm chances, WAA should lead to temperatures increasing \nback into the 80s to low 90s Saturday through Monday.",
